  "prediction": "The report discusses the current and former House rules regarding the statutory limit on the public debt, also known as the debt limit. The current rule, House Rule XXVIII, automatically engrosses and transmits a joint resolution suspending the debt limit when the House adopts the budget resolution. This means that the vote on the budget resolution is considered as the vote on the debt limit legislation. The new rule differs from the former one, known as the \"Gephardt rule,\" in that it sends the debt limit legislation to the Senate when the House adopts the budget resolution, rather than when the House and Senate agree to the budget resolution. Additionally, the new rule suspends the debt limit, while the former rule explicitly set a new debt limit. The report provides a historical background of the Gephardt rule, which was first adopted in 1979 and aimed to reduce the time and number of votes on debt limit legislation. The rule was suspended or repealed in 11 out of 31 years between 1980 and 2010, and during those years, the House originated 20 joint resolutions under the procedure. The Senate passed 16 of these joint resolutions, 10 without amendment and 6 with amendments. Overall, the report explains the current and former House rules regarding the debt limit and their differences, as well as the historical context and operation of the Gephardt rule.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the current and former House rules regarding the statutory limit on the public debt, also known as the debt limit. The current rule, House Rule XXVIII, automatically engrosses and transmits a joint resolution suspending the debt limit when the House adopts the budget resolution. This means that the vote on the budget resolution is considered as the vote on the debt limit legislation. The new rule differs from the former one, known as the \"Gephardt rule,\" in that it sends the debt limit legislation to the Senate when the House adopts the budget resolution, rather than when the House and Senate agree to the budget resolution. Additionally, the new rule suspends the debt limit, while the former rule explicitly set a new debt limit. The report provides a historical background of the Gephardt rule, which was first adopted in 1979 and aimed to reduce the time and number of votes on debt limit legislation. The rule was suspended or repealed in 11 out of 31 years between 1980 and 2010, and during those years, the House originated 20 joint resolutions under the procedure. The Senate passed 16 of these joint resolutions, 10 without amendment and 6 with amendments. Overall, the report explains the current and former House rules regarding the debt limit and their differences, as well as the historical context and operation of the Gephardt rule.",
